<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Trouble is brewing in the bond mar=
kets again,</strong> as investors grow more concerned about inflation, and =
signs that the US economy may be running too hot.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Government borrowing costs jumped yesterda=
y, and are rising again in Asia-Pacific markets this morning; a move that i=
s pulling down share prices.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Wednesday=E2=80=99s trigger was a surprisi=
ngly strong survey of US businesses - <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.t=
heguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEAX2I5fp6=
olyh5yUeClMls_iA5H3M6NWHZT3LmZgmzEL-002VodNT5qw7_rZSiNOvbGUbYaBrQY3Cvu6uoxf=
8VCXUthDzSWcBFRL18nvnMNYhJRZx6YpTu_QDUoU22LURRFRMm-HTbCA1oJ-XzQbTv6fEyCeU35=
GMVlGQiO5E62qxxH0uoqQWtbTlb1di8WKNNQmybNIJseqWDht04APEplGmw2hN139MV6_EGNPEO=
R3NLnzO9puFK9jj-PaB8gdwOAyzZUbUrfzmTuB0BXNU1vElufpc2eIyAJ7584u_kBxMurd-fxcA=
6xEIHq4PXAWydztC5Lbu5eqGbmMC8PeL0yp-aD024PAanMhWFWu5pRL-E9oL0Jj1BtI5TL9giKj=
2Zwg1rDBn-yRMx3UN9OBfS2/4ub/xhBWROsQSZCCllFVtCv2QA/h30/h001.ahZqPVya4HWTVej=
ZDNLXldwO0G_nqRSqOGJHqSt4fo8" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">as we covered yeste=
rday</a> - showing that activity was rising at the fastest pace in five yea=
rs, amid a surge in costs. This prompted a sell-off in US government bonds,=
 as traders calculated that this might prompt further rises in interest rat=
es to cool inflation.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Chris Weston, head of research at brokerag=
e Pepperstone, says: =E2=80=9C<em>With unemployment at 4.1% and growth runn=
ing above trend, the US economy is showing signs of modest overheating. The=
 Federal Reserve will therefore be firmly on notice. If the next inflation =
readings continue to print hot, policymakers may conclude that aggregate de=
mand needs to be brought lower through the blunt tool of higher interest ra=
tes.</em>=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Investors were also alarmed by a surprisin=
gly weak auction of US five-year bonds last night, which attracted low dema=
nd =E2=80=93 perhaps a sign that appetite for Treasury bonds is waning.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">With bond prices sliding, the yield (or ra=
te of return) on five-year US Treasuries was driven over 5% for the first t=
ime since 2007. Ten-year US Treasury yields surged over 5%, in their bigges=
t one-day move since Donald Trump=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CLiberation Day=E2=80=
=9D tariff announcement almost 18 months ago.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">These moves are rattling the wider global =
bond market (as US debt is the =E2=80=9Crisk-free=E2=80=9D asset used as a =
benchmark by global financial markets). Already today, yields on Japan=E2=
=80=99s benchmark bonds have hit their highest level in decades.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at <a hr=
ef=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Rl4YXL9V02VzVEMMcy=
TxP5NgPpyaQT1U-Y0mNCwBgXqGuLY66uTy7BL9F9xr99oUdp1dShY8WS1ZUOg4L259USGZIpgfC=
ej827mjhaIVq6Xn-lTV_rDpFin0cDbiyJGDUxHQgkFW7d-3UTKZ5wu375b1738pWTOCTIuBS5fH=
q0_6B19Jh1_PhL5310igDQcoq3fT_CvkI7sehZfpEl-1WA/4ub/xhBWROsQSZCCllFVtCv2QA/h=
31/h001.lsscMbkO_kQliEqbP-B87ubHUwu5mmaL11ZMaUnY0G8" style=3D"color: #0077B=
6;">Swissquote</a>, puts it simply: =E2=80=9C<em>Economic activity expanded=
 strongly while price pressures remained elevated. That=E2=80=99s the perfe=
ct combination for fuelling further rate-hike expectations.=E2=80=9D</em></=
p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Financial markets are now much more confid=
ent that the US Federal Reserve will raise interest rates rates at least on=
e more time this year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">According to <strong><a href=3D"https://ab=
link.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR2gvqcaXbp4rjM=
qpr6PFdtQXX3qYDW4_ySKai5ZNB2aO-3ki_qq60QiRWqN20DaYibHxltoIxOKgzDSWn92_fObzt=
b24oft9Pzsmz5Lp9wa--v9aGjxfHaRFiCmPxEM_AbXjjQiqbVvRgujURdj-PluuVwriXZnlzVoz=
5NYgBMGxpZB4o12qpezp8RTBiIBqNFnMDp7WyKEkz2ZDmVnpxxwoKSEcW8qykOulG2myD9h7WBk=
oACHiPeAHkQ-PAfV6oA/4ub/xhBWROsQSZCCllFVtCv2QA/h32/h001.ZfbUyBVFHy9sCc4-2Cn=
TFsVjuFkvS1odQ0WLMfS2OOs" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">CME Fedwatch</a></stron=
g>, there=E2=80=99s now a 55% chance that US rates are half a percentage po=
int higher by the end of December =E2=80=93 implying two quarter-point rate=
 rises (or one beefy hike!). That=E2=80=99s on top of the Fed=E2=80=99s hik=
e earlier this month.</p>
